WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.976
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.110
TARIQ SHAUKAT: I'm really
excited to introduce

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.850
Phil and David.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:16.300
And we'll have a conversation
about just the amazing success

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.670
that has been Pokemon Go.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.010
A number of you have
these Android phones

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.480
that we handed out.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.270
If you're not familiar
with Pokemon Go,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.540
you can actually find the
app on the phones, as well.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.790
Let me just ask the question.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:36.710
How many folks in the room
do not know Pokemon Go?

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:37.300
OK.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.440
We've got a handful here.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.930
How many folks in the
room are avid players?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:45.830
OK.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.410
A much larger group.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:49.140
There we go.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.700
Just some sheepish looks,
but there's no need for that.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.410
So Phil, can you tell
everyone just a little bit

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.950
about what Pokemon Go is?

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.230
It's obviously
this amazing piece

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.880
of technology, this amazing
game, but how do you

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.790
guys think about it Niantic?

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:05.790
PHIL KESLIN: So for those
of you who don't know,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.720
we started Niantic as a small
little startup-style project

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.250
within Google almost
five years ago.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.250
The goal of that
organization was

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.050
to create an
experience for people

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.750
to try and get them
off of their couches

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.190
and back in the real world
so they could experience

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.430
other people and new places.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.940
Create exploration
apps, ways for people

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.595
to discover new things
about their community.

00:01:30.595 --> 00:01:31.971
And what we found
along the way--

00:01:31.971 --> 00:01:33.511
so we created two
products initially.

00:01:33.511 --> 00:01:35.980
We created one called Field
Trip, which is a discovery app

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.000
to tell you interesting quirky
little facts about the world

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.350
around you, usually in the
location that you're standing,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.620
and then we created the
game called Ingress,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.270
which paired primarily
public artworks

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.840
with this real-world
location-based game.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.940
And it's essentially,
for those of you

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.900
that haven't played it, it's
a territory capture game,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.712
with the central point
being these public artworks.

00:02:01.712 --> 00:02:03.170
And what we found
along the way was

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.950
that people that were
actually playing the game

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.900
were enamored with
the opportunity

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.449
to explore and learn
about their location.

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.840
And that sort of invigorated us.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.120
Following an April
Fool's joke at Google,

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.480
capture all the Pokemon in
Google Maps, back in 2014,

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.610
we found what we thought
would be a great idea

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:23.990
for a real location-based game.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.720
And so we took the same
basic Pokemon-style

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.700
capture your Pokemon
activity, and mapped it

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:31.810
onto the real world.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.870
And lucky for us, the Pokemon
company was really receptive.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:39.480
For them, this was like
reimagining the Pokemon game.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.150
Actually, it was
what they originally

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.980
had imagined the
Pokemon game to be.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.040
So adding the augmented
reality features to it

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.664
was something really
special for them.

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:48.830
TARIQ SHAUKAT: That's great.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.910
Did you have any inkling when
you were creating the game

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.660
and architecting it
and what have you

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.200
about how successful
it would be?

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.850
PHIL KESLIN: We had
absolutely no idea

00:02:58.850 --> 00:02:59.975
how successful it would be.

00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:02.120
And I think we have a chart.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.080
So this chart shows
you what happened.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.974
There's an anecdote that we use.

00:03:09.974 --> 00:03:11.390
People have always
asked us, like,

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.270
when did you know this
was going to be huge.

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:15.530
And we knew within the
first six hours of launch

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.940
in Australia and New Zealand.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.480
And the reason why it was--
we launched Australia and New

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:26.200
Zealand with the expectation
that Australia and New Zealand

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.160
would be 4% of our
target audience.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.050
And that's the bottom
line of that graph.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.930
That's where we expected
the system to be operating.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.460
The red line above it was
our worst-case scenario.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.410
And that was 5x
our nominal usage.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.720
And within the first six hours
of our Australia-New Zealand

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.470
launch, we had already
hit 50% of our worst case.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:56.360
4% of our target was now
five-- was now half of 5x.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:00.690
And you can see the rocket
ship that happened after that.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.247
I think-- I mean,
I see Luke sitting

00:04:02.247 --> 00:04:03.330
in the audience over here.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.170
He's the director for customer
reliability engineering.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.520
And I mean, he was there
with us the whole time

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.460
riding this rocket
ship, although for us I

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.010
think it was more of a
hair's on fire kind of thing.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.113
It was a spectacular ride.

00:04:18.113 --> 00:04:20.029
Most people don't realize
that the engineering

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:24.960
team that made all this happen
on our side was four engineers.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.520
And we worked pretty much
nonstop for about six weeks

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.390
to make this a reality.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.907
And it was always--
not only-- for us,

00:04:32.907 --> 00:04:35.240
the surrealness was what was
happening in the real world

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.840
while this was
happening, but the fact

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.800
that every time we entered
a hangout, all of us

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.290
were just totally fried.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.870
And on the other side of the
wall, in the Google side,

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:46.340
there was about
40 people sitting

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.270
on this-- on the
other side of the--

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:51.140
it's like they just
really helped us feel

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.600
like we were gonna make it.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.240
So that was really kind of cool.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.406
TARIQ SHAUKAT: That's great.

00:04:55.406 --> 00:04:59.022
So Dave, I mentioned customer
reliability engineers before.

00:04:59.022 --> 00:05:01.480
And Phil just talked about the
40 people on the other side.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:08.462
But tell us the CRE view of
what Phil just talked about.

00:05:08.462 --> 00:05:10.350
DAVE RENSIN: Well
the CRE view is

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.400
like 20 minutes I think
after you launched

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.130
somebody on your team
called-- actually

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.510
called-- your account rep called
Simon, who called us and said,

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:22.249
um, just an early heads-up,
like we'll see how this goes,

00:05:22.249 --> 00:05:23.790
but this is kind of
trending-- what's

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.750
the word-- really upward.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:29.090
So we might want
to pay attention.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.750
And yeah, it didn't take
very long for everyone

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:35.450
to sort of figure out that,
OK, it's time to get going.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.710
The funny thing was we had
spent a long time planning

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.300
to announce this CRE
thing to the world.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.720
And it was-- and we had
gotten all the people we

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.980
needed into a team, and all sort
of the HR stuff you have to do.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.890
And we were gonna meet for
the first time July 6th

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.680
and start on this really
hairy problem we had planned.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.400
And then Pokemon Go
launched July 5th.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.340
And so we started a day early.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.550
And it was fine.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.900
But it was awesome
validation, right,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.622
because a thing like-- Tariq,
you and I have talked about it,

00:06:06.622 --> 00:06:11.290
I don't know, a thousand
times-- is this relationship

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.330
that people have with
their cloud vendors

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:15.550
is kind of unfair,
in a way, right.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.375
They are giving up control
of their infrastructure.

00:06:19.375 --> 00:06:21.750
And yeah, we're giving them
a lot of innovation and speed

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.830
and cost savings, and those
are all great things on paper,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.290
but at three in the morning
when there's trouble,

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.160
it's feel super unfair.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.400
And there has to be this
new model, kind of this-- I

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.691
like to think of it as sort
of this new social contract

00:06:33.691 --> 00:06:36.180
of shared fate and
shared responsibility,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.310
where, like, Googlers or
partners that we train

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:43.675
and certify, which is a big
part of the growth of this,

00:06:43.675 --> 00:06:45.550
you don't have to be
there with them at three

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.680
in the morning on the
pagers in the war rooms

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.530
taking mutual responsibility
for the success

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:54.520
not just of our cloud, but of
their production application.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.530
So this was the first one.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:00.030
And actually, as hard as it was,
it was an awesome experience.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.250
I wouldn't want to do, like,
four of them in a month,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.500
but it was a wonderful first--

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.670
TARIQ SHAUKAT: I think Phil's
with you on that, as well.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:05.900
PHIL KESLIN: Yeah.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:06.450
Oh, yeah.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.719
I mean, it was a great
experience for us.

00:07:08.719 --> 00:07:10.510
I mean, John has said
this multiple times--

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:17.270
he;s the CEO for Niantic-- that
I don't think that without--

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:21.040
I'm pretty sure without the
help of Google throughout this,

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.770
that I think the entire team
at Niantic would have imploded.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.820
We just didn't have the
engineering resources

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.370
to make this thing happen.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.950
And knowing-- and we
were testing limits

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.200
on not only our software,
but their software, as well.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.830
These were capabilities
in these systems that

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.040
had never been tested before.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:44.110
I mean, the largest Kubernetes
cluster in a multi-cluster-- I

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.130
remember trying to spin
that thing up one night,

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.340
and we were all just kind of
freaking out and terrified.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.320
But having everybody--
having experts

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.800
on both sides of the wall really
made this thing a success,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.850
and we're really, really
appreciative of it.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.802
TARIQ SHAUKAT: What impressed
me as I was hearing from Dave

00:08:01.802 --> 00:08:03.260
what was going on
behind the scenes

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:06.370
is how quickly we were able
to start pivoting together

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:08.596
from let's fix the
problems that we have

00:08:08.596 --> 00:08:09.970
and let's get the
scale out there

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:12.230
to let's anticipate
what's going to happen.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:14.340
Can you talk a little
bit about that?

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.090
DAVE RENSIN: Yeah.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.857
Luke actually has a great
saying which I love, which is--

00:08:17.857 --> 00:08:18.940
TARIQ SHAUKAT: Luke, wave.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:19.500
DAVE RENSIN: Yeah,
wave your hand.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:20.860
TARIQ SHAUKAT: Wherever Luke is.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:25.760
DAVE RENSIN: Which is that
forecasts are mostly useless,

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.920
but forecasting is
unbelievably helpful, right.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:34.030
Just the exercise of constantly
revising how you think things

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:34.780
are going to grow.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.140
And so, OK, Australia and
New Zealand, a little choppy

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.230
there for a minute.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.720
The US was a little bit better.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:41.760
We learned some stuff.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.159
It was a little better.

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:46.219
And but by the time we got
ready to do Japan, right,

00:08:46.219 --> 00:08:47.760
so we were kind of
ahead of the wave,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.540
by the time we got ready
to do Japan, which just had

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:53.130
to go right-- like in
the Japanese market,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.100
the tolerance for
hiccups is much lower

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:00.240
than in the US market,
or even in Europe.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.010
And we felt we had
kind of a good handle.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:03.520
We had gotten
ahead of that wave.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.720
And that was the thing, was the
act of forecasting and planning

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.380
and going through
the operational rigor

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.430
and really looking at the
scaling failure points,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.240
and then all the things we
kind of learned the last 15

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.140
years about what happens when
big systems go supernova fast,

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.980
and how do they break.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.830
That was, I think, the thing
that really kind of-- that

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:23.820
was the linchpin of it.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:24.832
PHIL KESLIN: Yeah.

00:09:24.832 --> 00:09:26.310
Yeah, it was awesome.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:29.740
I mean, it was-- I mean,
looking back on it,

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:35.172
the experience was-- at
times, it was disheartening.

00:09:35.172 --> 00:09:37.380
I mean, knowing that we're
only getting three or four

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.410
hours of sleep a
night, rolling out

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.960
of bed onto your computer--
I remember going to a swim

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.180
meet one morning and
then getting paged

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.060
and then waking
up Ed, who thought

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.110
he got paged by Google
SRE, but it was actually

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.200
me calling him, is just
how whacked out our world

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.887
was for about four to six weeks.

00:09:55.887 --> 00:09:57.470
But in the end, I
think because of all

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.380
of this and getting the
system really stable,

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.500
even with the growth
we had planned,

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.740
every single engineer
on that server team

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.060
was able to take a
two-week vacation.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.972
So we managed to survive
that, and the team

00:10:09.972 --> 00:10:11.180
has actually fully recovered.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.090
And I think the world
is a great place.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:19.582
TARIQ SHAUKAT: Well, it
really is a tremendous story

00:10:19.582 --> 00:10:20.530
of Pokemon Go.

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:23.670
I think the engineering
feat that you pulled off

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:28.290
of both combining the
maps and geo with cloud

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.490
with the game designs with
the integration of a really

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:34.560
iconic brand and then
scaling it to a level that

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.920
is really unprecedented in
terms of how quickly you did it,

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.330
it's really just
an amazing story,

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:42.350
and we appreciate
being part of it.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.290
PHIL KESLIN: I got two
statistics to share.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.000
The first one is that, at
the beginning of September--

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.500
I don't know what
the number is now,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:53.460
but we were over 500
million installs worldwide.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:56.000
And we aren't even
launched worldwide yet.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.250
And then the second one is that,
at the beginning of September,

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:05.750
Pokemon Go players have walked
4.6 billion kilometers, which

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:10.100
is more than half the distance
between Earth and Pluto,

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:10.750
which is--

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.500
TARIQ SHAUKAT: And there
are-- just for the record,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.290
there are Pokemon on
Pluto, is what I'm

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:15.700
hearing you say at the moment.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:16.340
PHIL KESLIN: Possibly.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:16.713
TARIQ SHAUKAT: OK.

00:11:16.713 --> 00:11:17.930
PHIL KESLIN: So I mean--

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:18.930
DAVE RENSIN: Legendary--

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.750
PHIL KESLIN: That's in 60 days,
so that's pretty phenomenal.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:24.970
That's just one of the
great things about working

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:28.790
on stuff like this, is seeing
people benefiting from it

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.857
and creating that
social interaction,

00:11:30.857 --> 00:11:32.440
which is really what
we set out to do.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.340
DAVE RENSIN: It's also
got to be the world's

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.840
most successful fitness app.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:35.920
TARIQ SHAUKAT: I was
just thinking that.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:36.300
That's right.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:36.760
DAVE RENSIN: I mean, half--

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:38.270
TARIQ SHAUKAT: One of the
